A||N||7||KRMFiero||Has anyone installed Fiero stores Poly cradle mounts?||||1||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||07-30-2001||10:41 PM||||well I ordered my Poly Cradle mounts Firday and they came in today, look nice!   how do i put them on? do i need anything from the only mounts? how do they stay in/hold together? what greese and stuff should I use?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||07-30-2001||11:10 PM||||You put them on by putting the car on jackstands, (don't have the stands support the cradle obviously) then support the end of the cradle that is coming down with a jack. Take one of the bolts out of the dogbone, then unbolt the end of the cradle you are going to drop. I used a torch to burn the front bushings out while leaving the sleeves intact. Some people use other methods and I don't think that you have to do that for the rear ones. Use any type of grease you want to put the new bushings back in. (some people will disagree on this and say only to use silicone grease.) Then jack the cradle back up, bolt it back in, and do the other end the same way.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||07-31-2001||03:41 AM||||There are a few methods to get the bushings out of your cradle.<P>1.  drill the outside of the bushing with an 1/8" drill bit to cut it free<P>2.  apply liquid nitrogen and shatter the bushing<P>3.  torch the sleeve holding the bushing  <P><BR>If you go with the torch, be careful.  When the rubber starts crackling and burning it will shoot out of there like a flaming roll of toilet paper.  It is a lot of fun, but very messy.  Wear old pants and shoes.<P>The bushings dramatically improved my 2M4s handling.  It is worth the work.<P>Do you have the poly control arm bushings?  They also help a lot!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||07-31-2001||09:52 AM||||I have homemade aluminum cradle bushings. They really helped the controllability of the rear end of the car.<BR>I would recommend hard cradle bushings or mounts as the first suspension upgrade to do to a Fiero.<BR>Hard rear control arm bushings are #2.<P>Only after your suspension geometry is reasonably stable will chassis tuning methods (springs, bars, shocks, etc.) give the greatest increase in driveability.||||reg||1
Z||000004||KRMFiero||07-31-2001||09:52 AM||||hehe this is going to smell bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I dont have the poly control arm bushings, maybe ill order a set of them this week to<P>Thanks<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||07-31-2001||11:30 AM||||It took me about 2 hours to burn my bushings out...I drilled holes, pulled, scooped it..what a mess.  Lots of nasty smelling smoke.  Yuk.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||07-31-2001||11:44 AM||||You only have to burn out the front cradle bushings. The rears pop out in a matter of seconds (if they aren't rusted in place :rolleyes <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>You might try running a hole saw down the axis of the front bushing. That will remove the center sleeve and some of the rubber with no burning. If your bushings are in really bad shape, the rest of the rubber may just pull out by hand with no burning necessary at all.<P>I did a complete set of control arm bushings this way, and only had to burn out two of the twelve.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-31-2001||08:00 PM||||The way I did mine:<P>1. make sure you can't cook anything with the torch. make some tin heat deflectors is need be. There are fuel lines up the passengers side on my 87SC. I don't remember where the lines run on all the others.<P>2. WEAR GOGGLES and Leather Gloves! Hot Rubber sticks to things and keeps burning! It can be like napalm that way.<P>3. put a pencil tipped propant torch so the flame blows thru the center sleeve. Heat it untill the rubber cooks enough to let go and pull it out with pliers.<P>4. chunk out the rubber with an old knife. One of those cheap "laser edge" steak knives with the fine seration works great.<P>5. use the torch only to cook off the remaining bits of rubber in the shell.<P>You'll need allot less heat on the outer shell this way.<P>I dind't grease mine at all. These ones don't move like sway bar or A arm bushings. They won't squeak. The grease might make them easier for some people to stuff into the front.<P>Dont forget to grease/antisieze the whole bolt in the front bushings! this will help keep them from siezing into the sleeves. Coating the inside of the sleeve isn't a bad idea. If these stick, you'll play hell getting them out.<P>Antisieze the rear bolts threads only. <P>If you replace these bolts I think they must be Grade 8. Not 100% sure at the moment with those.||||reg||1
